WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.040
- This was the size of
the smartphone market

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.843
before the iPhone came out.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.180
This was the size of the market

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.820
five years after the iPhone came out.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:09.670
Please don't pop.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.800
Product market categories tend to balloon

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.630
after Apple enters them.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.130
It really ballooned.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.503
But mixed reality headsets?

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.430
For the first time in eight years,

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.430
Apple's expected to enter
a new product category

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.430
with a new AR/VR like headset.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:32.010
It's set to look like
a pair of ski goggles,

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:34.260
and offer immersive and 3D versions

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.610
of FaceTime, Maps,
games, fitness, and more.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:41.040
It sounds cool, but also niche,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.360
at least right now.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.070
The market is smaller than any other

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:46.830
that Apple's decided to go
into in the last decade.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:47.670
- iPhone.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:48.503
The iPad.

00:00:48.503 --> 00:00:49.470
- Apple Watch.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:50.400
- AirPods.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.750
- So I dug up some data around

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.610
Apple's other product entries,

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.910
specifically the iPod and the Apple Watch,

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.410
to see if there's any hints

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.443
at whether this headset
will blow up or burst.

00:01:03.131 --> 00:01:05.464
♪ Ave Maria ♪

00:01:09.093 --> 00:01:10.290
- The balloons you're about to see

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.670
are not blown up to scale.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.100
The following numbers
were provided by IDC.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.890
Okay, let's look at the
iPod comparison first.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.800
In 2000, the year before
the iPod came out,

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.860
there were just 3.3
million global shipments

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.140
of compressed audio players.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:26.790
That number doesn't include Walkmans

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.860
and portable CD players.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.730
Then Steve Jobs introduced
the iPod with iTunes.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:33.810
- These are the four choices

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.250
for portable music right now.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.780
Boom, that's iPod.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:37.980
I happen to have one
right here in my pocket,

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:38.813
matter of fact.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.670
There it is right there.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.060
- And within four years,
the market had ballooned.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.803
It was eight times bigger.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.730
And Apple crushed the competition.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:51.993
Remember Rio?

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:53.843
Doubt it.

00:01:53.843 --> 00:01:56.460
The headset market is similar in two ways.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.470
Firstly, the smaller size.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:02.910
In 2022, 8.8 million AR
and VR headsets shipped,

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.590
with Meta's Quest 2 taking

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.750
the largest chunk of that market share.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.630
- Quest 2 is lighter, faster,

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.210
and has a better display
than the first generation.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:13.290
- Then there's also the fact

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.950
that digitizing music was a
nerdy thing before the iPod.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.210
You had to rip discs,

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:22.860
and this thing didn't exactly
scream "I'm super cool."

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.890
That's a lot like how wearing
one of these is today.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:26.880
But on the other hand,

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.010
easily digitizing and listening to music

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.140
on the go was a real problem

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:32.880
that needed to be solved.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.410
The iPod did it.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.450
While Meta has told us
that we all wanna live

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:39.000
in the Metaverse one day,
we don't quite know yet

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.270
what problem this nerd helmet solves.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:43.890
Okay, so maybe the headset market

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.290
is more like the smartwatch market.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.680
In 2014, the year before the
Apple Watch went on sale,

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.430
just shy of 7 million
smart watches shipped.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:54.480
Five years later,

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.720
the market was more
than 10 times the size.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.720
And yes, same thing happened
with the competition.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.430
Remember Jawbone?

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:03.527
Or Pebble?

00:03:03.527 --> 00:03:04.530
(balloon popping)

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:05.400
There was something different

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.290
about the growth of the
watch market though.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.330
It was slower to balloon than phones,

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:12.330
tablets, music players or earbuds.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.610
But the biggest similarity
between the headset

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:18.360
and the Apple Watch may be
its relation to this balloon,

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:19.950
the smartphone market.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.030
Thanks to the iPhone,

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:24.510
Apple has a massive 2
billion person install base,

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.720
that is people who already
own an Apple product.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.460
So I called up Gene Munster,

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.230
a longtime Apple analyst,

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:34.470
to ask him how he sees that
playing out with the headset.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.450
- Part of that is their ability

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.180
to basically create a market.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.640
Because they have this
big active install base,

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:44.250
it allows them to integrate a new product

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.760
within products that their
existing users already have.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.620
I think the balloon will take more time

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:52.170
to inflate than a typical Apple product,

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.810
but ultimately be a lot bigger

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:56.730
than a typical Apple product.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.290
Not as big as the iPhone

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.180
but this is a big balloon that

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:02.490
Apple needs to start inflating,

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:04.500
because if they don't,

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.960
Google and Meta and Samsung

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.290
are gonna find ways to
capture that opportunity.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:11.490
- Munster pointed out some ways

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:14.490
the headset strays from
the Apple playbook though.

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:17.520
The initial headset is
expected to be $3,000,

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:19.650
and aimed at software developers

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:21.780
versus normal consumers.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.750
To me, the headset market
goes one of two ways.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.180
The first is like the Apple Watch,

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.310
the headset gets cheaper and smaller,

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:32.310
and Apple gives us all some
reason to wear this thing,

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:34.230
not only in our windowless basements

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:36.900
but actually out on the streets.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.000
We need to understand
that this could be better

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.480
than our phones or laptops at something.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.004
And well, the second scenario is.

00:04:45.004 --> 00:04:48.540
(balloon whooshing)

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.760
It would certainly be a new one for Apple.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.718
World's largest whoopee cushion.

00:04:52.718 --> 00:04:54.876
(balloon whooshing)

